Fate Transport Simulation App Instruction Manual 

1. Before using this software 
This app is a compiled COMSOL application for simulating sedimentation of agglomerated particles. 
COMSOL Multiphysics® is a physics simulation software product developed by COMSOL AB. There is no 
license required to run this app.  
If this app is the version that is prepared with the COMSOL Runtime in it, it is already ready to run. If the 
version is prepared without the COMSOL Runtime, then you need to follow the instruction provided by 
the party that provided the app. You need to open the link that the app provider gave you and 
download the Runtime and keep it in your local machine. You need to install the Runtime only once.  

2. Purposes 
This software can be used to analyze sedimentation phenomena of agglomerated particles in a solution 
represented as one dimensional problem. It computes the concentration of the agglomerated particles, 
which can derive various quantities such as mass, mass fraction, surface concentration, and number 
concentration.  

3. Features 
This app has the following features:  
1.  File import for materials for particles and solutions  
2.  File import for all simulation parameters 
3.  File import for particle diameter distribution 
4.  Predefined material data 
5.  Geometrical parametric sweep 
6.  Material parametric sweep 
7.  Export the computed plot data 
8.  Output an analysis report 

4. How to Use the Software 
Buttons 
At the top of the graphical user interface (GUI), there are four buttons.  

Run : Click this button to run this app. 
Reset : Click this button to reset the parameters to default. 
Report : Click this button to generate a report. 
Manual : Click this button to open the instruction manual. 
Save As : Click this button if you want to generate a COMSOL Multiphysics file (You will need a license to 
open the file.)  

Geometry section 

You can specify your test tube geometry in one dimension. Column height  is the height of the test tube. 
Specify the height in millimeters. Bottom cell height  stands for the height of the bottom part of the tube 
where you want to calculate the local average concentration and mass. Specify it in microns .  
If you want to sweep these numbers, check Sweep checkbox off and enter the start and end values by a 
floating number, and the number of intervals by a positive integer. If you have entered an invalid value 
(a start value larger than the end value, for example), an error message will pop up or the app just 
doesn’t do anything.  
Note that you can sweep only one parameter at a time.  







Particle section 

For the parameters for materials, you can do three types of simulations, which are a single run, a 
parametric sweep, and a simulation for the size distribution. If no parameter is chosen as a sweeping 
parameter, the app runs for a single set of parameters that are shown in the parameters pane. If you 
want to sweep a parameter, check the Sweep checkbox off. The initial condition can be set here. There 
are predefined materials that you can choose from a pull-down menu, which are ZnO, TiO2, CeO2, SiO2, 
Fe2O3.  
Material properties can be imported as a text file. The file format is the following: 
1st  column  2nd  column  
rp Radius  in nm  
rho  Bulk density  in g/cm^3  
Rhop  Effective density  in g/cm^3  

Example: 
rp 491.05 [nm] 
rho 7.215 [g/cm^3] 
rhop 1.42 [g/cm^3] 

You can add the third column for your reference. The app does’t read the first column and columns after 
the second column.  
If you want to include the particle size distribution data to study the multi-particle effect, you can import 
the distribution data file. The file format is the following: 
1st  column  2nd  colum n 3rd  column  
Dia. 1 for bin 1  Dia. 2  for bin 1  Fraction  for bin 1  
Dia. 1 for bin 2 Dia. 2  for bin 2 Fraction  for bin 2 
….  …. …. 

Di. 1 f or bin N  Dia. 2 for bin N  Fraction for bin N  

An example of actual data looks like the following: 

270 285 0.030066010658415 
285 360 0.0582609605677011 
360 460 0.0982306893292635 
460 550 0.214849389468931 
550 690 0.151749478687966 
690 865 0.11045294099696 
865 1094 0.0692087903672716 
1094 1369 0.12007765344026 
1369 1769 0.147104086483232   

If you have imported this data file, you will see a distribution plot that looks like:  

The unit of the diameters must be in nanometers . The fraction does not need to be normalized. This app 
will always normalize the imported data. You cannot use the data directly from the DLS measurement. 
You have to “digitize” the original data into a histogram to approximate the original DLS data.  
Note: The histogram consists of some bins containing the fraction of the particle diameter for the range 
corresponding to the bin.  

Note: The diameter ranges must be contiguous between the bins.   
Note: When particle size distribution is selected, it is a single run. No parametric sweep is allowed. 

The bulk density and effective density can be swept in the same way.  

Solution section 

For solutions, you can do the same as the material parameters. You can import a text file that contains 
the viscosity and the density of the solution. The file format and an example for solutions are the 
following: 
1st  column  2nd  column  
musol  Viscosity  in Pa*s  
rho sol  Density  in g/cm^3  

musol 0.001 [Pa*s] 
rhosol 1.0 [g/cm^3] 

Predefined solution data are prepared for RPMI + 10% FBS at 37 degC and 22 degC.  
Note: This app does not simulate any temperature dependence at the version 1.0. The material 
properties are supposed to be the value at the temperature you desire to simulate for.   

Simulation section 

You can specify the computation time and time increment in hours . 

Note: The time increment is used to store the solutions, not the actual computational time step, which is 
automatically chosen by the software.  
You can change the number of mesh for the entire column height less the bottom part and that for the 
bottom part. It is recommended to not change these settings but there may be some occasions where 
you want to try different mesh sizes. Particles with larger densities, larger size distributions are such 
cases. Computation tends to become difficult and unstable in such cases because the particles sink very 
quickly. In such cases, you can try to increase the number of mesh elements for the bottom part. The 
upper part does not matter much. If computation takes unusually a long time, check the density and the 
size and try to run lighter and smaller particles and increase these values gradually. If the computation 
breaks down at a point, that’s where you may need to increase the number of mesh elements. If the 
increase of the mesh elements didn’t work, it is the limitation of this software.  

All Parameters section 

You can import a text file that contains all parameters including the geometry, particle, solution, and 
simulation time. By importing the file, you can define all the parameters you need at once. 

Results section 



There are six plots in the window: 
1.  Mass concentration spatial distribution along the column: c(t,x) 
If a parametric sweep is selected, the result for only the last time step for each parameter value will 
be displayed. 
